  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty minor coastal flooding will be possible \nwith the this morning's high tide cycle. \n\nThere will be the potential for some spotty minor coastal \nflooding with this morning's high tide cycle for places along \nthe Atlantic coast and Delaware Bay. No Coastal Flood Advisory \nis in effect at this time.\n\nNo tidal flooding concerns expected along the Chesapeake Bay.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Surge of warmth and humidity through Wednesday \nahead of a cold front.\n\nA frontal zone draped to our north today will result in surface\nlow pressure riding along it tonight and Wednesday. This will \npull a cold front across our area by later Wednesday. Ahead of \nthis feature, southerly flow will boost the low-level warm air \nadvection and this results in much warmer air surging into our \narea through Wednesday. High temperatures will near 80 degrees \nfor many areas today, then into the low to even some mid 80s on\nWednesday. In addition, dew points will increase back through \nthe 60s Tuesday and Wednesday, with them peaking on Wednesday \n(near 70F for parts of the area) ahead of the aforementioned \nfront. This will result in a much more humid feel over the next \ncouple days, with it being the most humid on Wednesday (heat \nindex values should approach 90F for areas from about \nPhiladelphia south and east; away from the coast). There does \nnot appear to be all that much in the way of forcing for ascent \nwith the front as it arrives by later Wednesday, therefore just \na low chance (20-30 percent) for showers as of now. A rumble of\nthunder is possible along the eastern shore into Delaware where\nthere may be a little more instability, but any coverage should\nbe isolated at most. The dew points will fall from north to \nsouth later Wednesday night into Thursday as the front settles \nsouthward and the winds turn out of the north and northeast. The\nfront however may not completely clear our region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A strong cold front Friday brings an end to the\nwarmth as much colder air arrives for the weekend.\n\nA potent upper-level trough is forecast to arrive across the East \nduring Friday, then gradually lift out over the weekend. This will \ndrive surface low pressure just north of our area Friday with an \nassociated strong cold front sweeping through.\n\nA mild and increasingly moist air mass ahead of this feature will \nprovide some instability as the strong cold front arrives on Friday. \nLarge scale forcing for ascent will increase with the arrival of the \nrobust upper-level trough and cold front, therefore showers will \nincrease for a time (peaking on Friday). Some showers though look to \ndevelop Thursday night ahead of the main cold front. Some \nthunderstorms cannot be ruled out given the strength of the system, \nhowever this will ultimately depend on the magnitude of the \ninstability and timing of the cold front. In the wake of the cold \nfront, strong cold air advection occurs and this will result in much \ncolder temperatures for the weekend. As of now, high temperatures \nboth Saturday and Sunday look to struggle to get out of the 50s \n(colder in the higher terrain). A gusty wind Saturday due to cold \nair advection and a tightened pressure gradient will add to the \nchill. If the wind drops off enough Saturday night, some frost or \nfreezing temperatures will become a concern for portions of our \narea. The air mass then starts to modify early next week.",
